<title>USA: U.S. Q2 NON-FARM PRODUCTIVITY ROSE 0.6 PCT.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q2 NON-FARM PRODUCTIVITY ROSE 0.6 PCT.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-08-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor estimates of U.S. productivity/costs, seasonally adjusted annual percent changes from prior quarters, on chain weights basis (1992=100):</p>
<p>    -	     - Productivity -	  - - -Output- - -</p>
<p>    -	     Q2-P    Q1    Q4	  Q2-P    Q1    Q4</p>
<p> NonFarm	   0.6   1.4   1.8	   2.3   5.6   5.4</p>
<p> Manufacturing   3.2   2.5   3.7	   4.4   5.4   4.3</p>
<p> Durable Goods   5.1   3.0   2.1	   7.1   8.3   2.8</p>
<p> NonDurables     0.9   2.7   5.5	   1.1   2.1   5.9</p>
<p> Total Business  0.7   1.8   1.9	   2.4   5.9   5.4</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.</p>
<p>    -	     - - -Hours- - -	Hourly Compensation</p>
<p>    -	     Q2-P    Q1    Q4	  Q2-P    Q1    Q4</p>
<p> NonFarm	   1.7   4.2   3.6	   3.1   4.5   3.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing   1.2   2.8   0.6	   2.7   4.4   1.9</p>
<p> Durable Goods   1.9   5.2   0.7	   2.3   4.7   0.5</p>
<p> NonDurables     0.2  -0.6   0.4	   3.4   3.4   4.3</p>
<p> Total Business  1.7   4.0   3.4	   3.0   4.4   3.3</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.  </p>
<p>    -	 Real Hourly Compensation    Unit Labor Costs</p>
<p>    -	     Q2-P    Q1    Q4	  Q2-P    Q1    Q4</p>
<p> NonFarm	   2.0   2.1  -0.1	   2.4   3.1   1.5</p>
<p> Manufacturing   1.6   2.0  -1.4	  -0.4   1.8  -1.7</p>
<p> Durable Goods   1.2   2.3  -2.7	  -2.7   1.7  -1.6</p>
<p> NonDurables     2.3   1.0   0.9	   2.5   0.7  -1.1</p>
<p> Total Business  1.9   1.9  -0.1	   2.3   2.5   1.4</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast Q2 non-farm productivity to rise 0.7 percent and unit labor costs to be up 2.7 percent.</p>
<p>Compared to the second quarter of 1996, second quarter 1997 non-farm productivity rose 0.7 percent, output rose 3.6 percent, hours rose 2.9 percent, hourly compensation increased 3.4 percent, real hourly compensation rose 1.0 percent and unit labor costs increased 2.7 percent.</p>
<p>Non-farm productivity for the first quarter was revised to a gain of 1.4 percent from a previously reported 2.6 percent increase, the department said.</p>
